    "Bxc3+"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here comes a famous concession — capturing on c3 and giving up the fianchettoed bishop. At first sight it looks reckless, surrendering a prized piece and tearing a hole on the long diagonal — unthinkable to a beginner — yet the structural damage it inflicts on the opponent more than justifies it.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 690,
   "fen": "r1bqk1nr/pp1ppp1p/2n3p1/8/2P5/2P5/P1N1PPPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "bxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 711,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp1ppp1p/2n2np1/8/2P5/2P5/P1N1PPPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 1 8",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "And the supposed dark-square weakness can't actually be exploited, because the knight hops out to f6 to cover the sensitive squares. The trade is all upside once the defence is properly arranged.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 715.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp1ppp1p/2n2npB/8/2P5/2P5/P1N1PPPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 2 8",
   "line": [
    "Bh6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Even a bishop swung out to h6 achieves nothing here — it just sits there, doing no real work, while the opponent's shattered pawns quietly begin to collapse. Menacing placement without a threat behind it is empty."
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 718.5,
